This document was prepared using Ubuntu 25.04.

===============================
DEVELOPMENT SYSTEM SETUP
===============================

---------------------------
--- install android/sdk ---
---------------------------

# download android sdk command line tools from here:
https://developer.android.com/studio#command-line-tools-only

# unzip the cmdlinetools
mkdir -p ~/android/sdk
cd ~/android/sdk
unzip ~/Downloads/commandlinetools-linux-13114758_latest.zip

# move the cmdlinetools, using the required directory structure
mv cmdline-tools latest
mkdir cmdline-tools
mv latest cmdline-tools/latest

# update bashrc exports
export ANDROID_HOME=$HOME/android/sdk
export PATH=$PATH:$ANDROID_HOME/cmdline-tools/latest/bin:$ANDROID_HOME/platform-tools

# install sdk packages
# - note that the ndk package is installed automatically on first build of azApp
sdkmanager "platform-tools" "platforms;android-35" "build-tools;35.0.0"

# sdkmanager --help  (summary)
    Usage:
      sdkmanager [--uninstall] [<common args>] [--package_file=<file>] [<packages>...]
      sdkmanager --update [<common args>]
      sdkmanager --list [<common args>]
      sdkmanager --list_installed [<common args>]
      sdkmanager --licenses [<common args>]
      sdkmanager --version

    With --install (optional), installs or updates packages.
        By default, the listed packages are installed or (if already installed)
        updated to the latest version.

-----------------------
--- ubuntu packages ---
-----------------------

These packages are needed when building the linux version of ezApp.
Linux ezApp is used for development and test.

sudo apt update
sudo apt install openjdk-17-jdk
sudo apt install libsdl3-dev       # available on Ubuntu 25.04
sudo apt install libsdl3-ttf-dev   # available on Ubuntu 25.04

===============================
BUILD AND INSTALL EZAPP ON ANDROID
===============================

Steps:
- ./setup
- make build_debug
- make install_debug   (first connect adb)

The ./setup script does the following:
- downloads SDL3 and SDL3_ttf source code
- extract SDL3 source code
- creates template SDL3 build directory, using script 
    /build-scripts/create-android-project.py 
- extracts SDL3_ttf source to
    SDL3-3.2.28/build/org.sthaid.ezApp/app/jni/SDL3_ttf-3.2.2/
- downloads external SDL3_ttf packages by using the download.sh script 
    SDL3-3.2.28/build/org.sthaid.ezApp/app/jni/SDL3_ttf-3.2.2/external/download.sh
- update/add files; 
  . some files in SDL3-3.2.28/build/org.sthaid.ezApp/app are replaced with 
    symlinks to the files in ./setup_files
  . the original files are saved to ./setup_files/orig
  . the setup_files/diff_against_orig script is available to view the changes
- ezApp source code files are symlinked to app/jni/src
- ezApp assets directory symlink is created in app/src/main

===============================
ADB SETUP
===============================

Google AI says:
  "ADB (Android Debug Bridge) is a versatile command-line tool 
   from the Android SDK that lets your computer communicate with an Android
   device for debugging, app management, file transfer, and running shell commands"

Adb executable is here: android/sdk/platform-tools/adb

----------------------------------------
--- setup device to be used with adb ---
----------------------------------------

Enable developer mode by tapping build number 8 times.
  Settings -> About Phone -> Software Information -> Build Number

Go to Developer Options , and enable
- Stay Awake
- USB Debugging

Connect USB data cable. And select 'allow access' on the device.
- verify connection: 'adb shell'

To use adb over Wifi, without USB cable ...

With USB cable plugged in, run cmd 
- adb tcpip 5555
- should see message "restarting in TCP mode port: 5555"

Disconnect USB cable.

Issue the adb connect cmd:
- adb connect 192.168.1.243:5555
- verify using 'adb shell'

Suggestions:
- plug phone in with power cable, to disable sleeping while charging.
- configure router to assign a static IP address to your Android device
- configure your Android device so it doesn't change its MAC address,
  when using a trusted router:
    Connections -> Wifi -> Gear -> View More -> MAC Address Type -> Pnone MAC

===============================
ADB COMMAND EXAMPLES
===============================

adb help

adb logcat --help
adb logcat -c -b all                      # clear Android log
adb logcat -s SDL SDL/APP AndroidRuntime  # view logs from SDL SDL/APP AndroidRuntime components

adb shell  # run remote shell
adb shell getprop ro.product.cpu.abi  # get Application Binary Interface

adb kill-server   # kill and start adb server
adb start-server

adb tcpip 5555    # restart Android Debug Bridge Daemon listening on TCP port 5555
adb connect 192.168.1.205:5555  # connect to device
adb devices       # list connected devices

===============================
ANDROID VERSION NUMBERS
===============================

References:
- https://apilevels.com/
- Google: "list of android architecture versions and API levels"

                Android(*)
Codename        Version     API Level   Year    (subset)
--------        -------     ---------   ----
Baklava         16          36          2025
Nougat          7.0         24          2016
Lollipop        5           21          2015

(*) Android Version found here:: Setup -> About phone -> Software information

From Google AI ...

Android SDK Version (minSdkVersion, targetSdkVersion)
- These are attributes used in your app's main build configuration 
  (like a Gradle file in Android Studio) to declare compatibility
  with specific API levels. 
- minSdkVersion: The minimum API level your application can run on.
  Devices running an Android version with an API level lower than your
  minSdkVersion will not be able to install your app from Google Play.
- targetSdkVersion: The API level your app was tested against and is 
  fully compatible with.
- compileSdkVersion: The API level of the Android platform version you
  compile your app against. You should always use the latest stable 
  version for this to access new features and improvements. 

APP_PLATFORM 
- a variable used specifically within the Android NDK (Native Development
  Kit) build system (primarily in the Application.mk file for ndk-build). 
- It declares the Android API level against which your native C/C++ code
  is compiled. For native code, the APP_PLATFORM essentially acts as the
  minimum required API level for your native libraries, similar to how
  minSdkVersion works for the overall application.
- If not specified in Application.mk, ndk-build typically defaults to a
  minimum API level supported by the NDK itself or tries to infer it from
  your minSdkVersion set in the app's manifest/Gradle file. 

NDK Version:
- explicit configuration, in build.gradle:
        android {
            ...
            ndkVersion "23.1.7779620"
        }
- Automatic Selection:
  If you do not specify an ndkVersion, the Android Gradle Plugin (AGP)
  will automatically select a default version that it is known to be
  compatible with

===============================
SDL3 ANDROID BUILD DIRECTORY STRUCT
===============================

├── app
│   ├── jni
│   │   ├── SDL                   : SDL3 code
│   │   ├── SDL3_ttf-3.2.2        : SDL3_ttf code
│   │   └── src                   : app C-language code
│   └── src
│       └── main
│           ├── assets            : files that can be accessed using SDL_LoadFile
│           ├── java
│           │   └── org
│           │       ├── libsdl
│           │       │   └── app   : main java code, SDLActivity.java ...
│           │       └── sthaid
│           │           └── ezApp : EzappActivity.java (stub file)
│           └── res               : icons and values
└── gradle
    └── wrapper

===============================
EZAPP ADDITIONS TO LIBSDL JAVA CODE
===============================

SDL3 java code changes:
- additions to SDLActivity.java
- new files:
  - ezapp_fgsvc.java 
  - ezapp_utils.jave

Capabilities added:
- start/stop foreground mode: when foreground is enabled, ezApp continues
  to run when the app is minimized or the android device is sleeping
- get location: latitude, longitude, altitude
- text to speech
- flashlight control

These added capabilities can be invoked from C language, by using NDK
routines such as CallVoidMethod. File src/utils.cpp provides routines,
such as "void util_text_to_speech(char *text)".

